{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a9905f6a-1bfb-4cec-9a2f-9c262014621f","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Standing waves form when two identical waves travel in opposite directions and interfere.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Standing waves"},{"op":"equation","id":"super","region":"A2","latex":"y=A\\sin(kx-\\omega t)+A\\sin(kx+\\omega t)"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a9905f6a-1bfb-4cec-9a2f-9c262014621f","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Using a trigonometric identity, their sum becomes a fixed pattern times an oscillation.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"identity","region":"A3","latex":"y=2A\\sin(kx)\\cos(\\omega t)"},{"op":"text","id":"meaning","anchor":{"el":"identity","side":"below","gap":0.08},"content":"Shape stays in place; amplitude varies with time"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"What causes the standing wave pattern?","expected_gist":"interference of opposite-traveling identical waves"}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a9905f6a-1bfb-4cec-9a2f-9c262014621f","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Nodes never move, while antinodes oscillate with maximum amplitude.","ops":[{"op":"axes","id":"waveaxes","region":"right","x":{"min":0,"max":6.28,"label":"position x"},"y":{"min":-2,"max":2,"label":"displacement y"}},{"op":"curve","id":"pattern","axes_id":"waveaxes","expr":"2*sin(x)","domain":[0,6.28]},{"op":"text","id":"nodes","region":"B2","content":"Nodes: zero amplitude"},{"op":"text","id":"antinodes","region":"B3","content":"Antinodes: maximum amplitude"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a9905f6a-1bfb-4cec-9a2f-9c262014621f","step":{"id":"s4","script":"For a string fixed at both ends, only wavelengths fitting these boundary conditions can occur.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"modes","region":"left","latex":"L=n\\frac{\\lambda}{2},\\quad n=1,2,3,\\ldots"},{"op":"equation","id":"frequency","region":"left","latex":"f_n=n\\frac{v}{2L}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
